````````
==========================================================
Failed SSH Login Detection
Signal vs Noise: Measuring Alert False-Positive Rates in Splunk
==========================================================
Groups failed SSH logins by source IP and 1-minute window.
Tune the where count threshold based on your own false-positive testing (see /report).
Run each query separately in Splunk Search.
````````


````````Query 1: All failed-login windows per source IP```
index=* host=kali "Failed password"
| rex "from (?<src_ip>\d+\.\d+\.\d+\.\d+)"
| bin _time span=1m
| stats count by src_ip, _time
| sort -_time


```````Query 2: Detection rule, windows with 3 or more failures (tuned threshold)```
index=* host=kali "Failed password"
| rex "from (?<src_ip>\d+\.\d+\.\d+\.\d+)"
| bin _time span=1m
| stats count by src_ip, _time
| where count >= 3
| sort -_time


``````Query 3: Failures and successes per source IP (many fails then a success suggests brute force)```
index=* host=kali ("Failed password" OR "Accepted password")
| rex "from (?<src_ip>\d+\.\d+\.\d+\.\d+)"
| bin _time span=1m
| stats count(eval(searchmatch("Failed password"))) as fails,
        count(eval(searchmatch("Accepted password"))) as success
        by src_ip, _time
| sort -_time


`````Query 4: Total failed attempts per source IP (quick overview)```
index=* host=kali "Failed password"
| rex "from (?<src_ip>\d+\.\d+\.\d+\.\d+)"
| stats count by src_ip
| sort -count
`````
